On Mon, Sep 14, 2009 at 4:41 PM, Stela Selckiku <selckiku@gmail.com> wrote:
=0AOn Thu, Sep 3, 2009 at 11:23 PM, Luke Bergen<lukeabergen@gmail.com> wrot=
e:
=0A> I don't know lojban well enough yet to be 100% sure, but I believe you=
've
=0A> just said (in a nutshell):
=0A
=0A
=0ASorry, I don't know why I felt compelled to answer in Lojban, but it
=0Adoes seem like you understood what I said. :)
=0A
=0AThere are a lot of challenges to raising someone to speak a new
=0Alanguage, but it seems like d'Armond Speers had extra obstacles in the
=0AKlingon of that time, which was inadequate to the task and too
=0Aconservative to adjust quickly. =A0Today's Lojban is always inventing
=0Anew words, for instance, and we could easily supply words for a new
=0Aneed, while the Klingon community is very restrictive about when new
=0Awords can enter their language.
=0A
=0AThe concept of "jbojbe", children who speak Lojban from birth, has
=0Abeen discussed for a long time. =A0I think it's about time we started
=0Adiscussing it just a little bit more seriously, and seeing what we
=0Acould do as a community to help those who might want to teach Lojban
=0Ato their children. =A0The state of the language and the community and of
=0Atechnology are all I think heading towards a place where a jbojbe
=0Acould have contact with and support from a lot of Lojbanists, such as
=0Athrough recordings or video phone, and I think we should also start
=0Aworking for instance on songs and videos and stories designed
=0Aespecially for the jbojbe.
=0A
=0ALearning a language is difficult for anyone, even a child. =A0My hope is
=0Athat the jbojbe will feel that teaching them Lojban is not just an
=0Aexperiment, but that we have actually prepared a worthwhile place and
=0Arole for them in our community.
=0A
=0Ami'e la stela selckiku
=0Amu'o
